<section class="hero"><div class="eyebrow">Real life. Real food. Real budgets.</div><h1>Feed your people. Keep your budget.</h1><p>Easy family meals, no-fuss meal plans, and grocery-saving ideas for busy households. Good food doesn't have to cost a fortune.</p><a class="btn" href="#recipes">Find your next dinner ↓</a></section>
<section class="wrap" id="recipes"><h2 class="section-title">🍝 What’s cooking?</h2><p>Simple ideas for the nights when everyone is hungry and nobody knows what to make.</p><div class="cards"><article class="card"><div class="emoji">🍅</div><span class="tag">Budget-friendly</span><h3>One-Pot Tomato Pasta</h3><p>Pasta, canned tomatoes, garlic, broth, and a little cheese make a comforting dinner with minimal cleanup.</p><a href="#pasta">Get the recipe ↓</a></article><article class="card"><div class="emoji">🌮</div><span class="tag">Family favorite</span><h3>Build-Your-Own Taco Night</h3><p>Put out seasoned beans or meat, tortillas, rice, lettuce, and toppings so everyone can make their own plate.</p></article><article class="card"><div class="emoji">🥣</div><span class="tag">Use what you have</span><h3>Mix-and-Match Bowls</h3><p>Start with rice or potatoes, add leftover protein and vegetables, then finish with your favorite sauce.</p></article></div></section>
<section class="wrap orange" id="pasta" style="border-radius:22px"><h2 class="section-title">🍅 One-Pot Tomato Pasta</h2><p><strong>Serves:</strong> about 4 · <strong>Time:</strong> about 25 minutes</p><div class="split"><div><h3>Ingredients</h3><ul><li>12 oz pasta</li><li>1 can (14–15 oz) diced tomatoes</li><li>3 cups broth or water, plus more if needed</li><li>2 cloves garlic, minced, or 1 tsp garlic powder</li><li>1 tsp Italian seasoning</li><li>Salt and pepper to taste</li><li>Grated cheese, optional</li></ul></div><div><h3>Make it</h3><ol><li>Add pasta, tomatoes, broth, garlic, and seasoning to a large pot.</li><li>Bring to a boil, then simmer, stirring often, until pasta is tender. Add a splash of water if needed.</li><li>Season to taste and top with cheese.</li><li>Add frozen spinach, leftover vegetables, or cooked beans if you have them.</li></ol></div></div><p class="note">Cooking times vary by pasta shape. Make sure the pasta is fully cooked and follow package guidance where needed.</p></section>
<section class="wrap" id="savings"><h2 class="section-title">💸 The grocery-saving corner</h2><p>Save money without making dinner feel like a second job.</p><div class="cards"><article class="card"><div class="emoji">📝</div><h3>Shop your kitchen first</h3><p>Check the fridge, freezer, and pantry before making your list. Plan meals around ingredients you already own.</p></article><article class="card"><div class="emoji">🔁</div><h3>Give ingredients two jobs</h3><p>Roast extra vegetables or cook extra rice so tomorrow's lunch is halfway done.</p></article><article class="card"><div class="emoji">🛒</div><h3>Compare unit prices</h3><p>Check the price per ounce or pound, not just the package price. Bigger isn't always cheaper.</p></article></div><div class="card" style="margin-top:20px"><h3>Quick weekly grocery reset</h3><p>□ Check pantry and freezer<br>□ Choose 3–5 dinners<br>□ Write a list by store section<br>□ Check store sales and coupons<br>□ Plan one leftover night<br>□ Set a grocery spending limit</p></div></section>
<section class="wrap pink" id="mealplan" style="border-radius:22px"><h2 class="section-title">📅 Your easy 3-dinner plan</h2><div class="cards"><article class="card"><h3>Monday</h3><p>One-pot tomato pasta with a side of vegetables.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Tuesday</h3><p>Taco bowls using beans or ground meat, rice, and your favorite toppings.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Wednesday</h3><p>Use-it-up bowls with leftover protein, vegetables, and potatoes or rice.</p></article></div><p><strong>Money-saving move:</strong> Use overlapping ingredients across meals to cut waste and avoid buying one-off items.</p></section>
